# BiguaSim T2 SITL overrides for the BlueROV2 (ArduSub) — NAME VALUE format
#
# sim_vehicle.py -v ArduSub already loads ArduPilot's own generic
# Tools/autotest/default_params/sub.parm as a base before this file is
# applied on top (same --defaults-then---add-param-file layering already
# confirmed live for the ArduCopter/Rover cases) — so, like
# t2_biguasim_blueboat.parm, this starts close to empty and only grows once a
# live test run shows a concrete need (motor-thrust mapping, EKF/depth
# tuning, etc.).
#
# One exception, NOT left for live discovery: the scheduler loop-rate fix.
# Confirmed by reading ardupilot/libraries/AP_Scheduler/AP_Scheduler.cpp:44-47
# directly that SCHEDULER_DEFAULT_LOOP_RATE is 400 for ArduSub, same as
# Copter (Rover is the outlier at 50) — i.e. ArduSub has the exact same
# loop-rate mismatch against BiguaSim's real ~120-135Hz delivery rate that
# broke DjiMatrice arming until t2_biguasim.parm's SCHED_LOOP_RATE fix. Set
# proactively here, matching t2_sitl_run_bluerov2.sh's -A "--rate 120".
SCHED_LOOP_RATE 120

# Second exception, also NOT left for live discovery: the same EKF/GPS
# health relaxation already proven necessary for the DjiMatrice
# (t2_biguasim.parm's FS_EKF_ACTION/EK3_CHECK_SCALE/EK3_GLITCH_RAD) — found
# live to block ArduSub too, in a way that's easy to misdiagnose. Confirmed
# by reading ArduSub/system.cpp directly: Sub::ekf_position_ok() (called
# from Sub::position_ok(), which ModeGuided::init() requires to be true)
# returns false once armed unless AP_AHRS::Status::CONST_POS_MODE is clear —
# i.e. the EKF must actively be trusting its configured position source
# (GPS, the framework default for EK3_SRC1_POSXY, unchanged here). BiguaSim's
# synthetic GPS produces the same transient jump/glitch behavior documented
# in t2_biguasim.parm (fires around AHRS init, not just at the bridge-deck
# level) — without this relaxation, the EKF intermittently falls back to
# CONST_POS_MODE, position_ok() stays false, and every GUIDED mode-set
# request is silently rejected by the firmware (MAVROS still reports
# mode_sent: true — that only means the command was transmitted, not
# accepted — confirmed live: mode stayed MANUAL, guided: false, no error
# surfaced anywhere in ardupilot_interface, mission, or mavros logs).
FS_EKF_ACTION 0
EK3_CHECK_SCALE 200
EK3_GLITCH_RAD 5

# Third exception: root-caused via the dataflash log (mavlogdump.py --types
# MSG, more reliable than the live MAVLink channel, which struggled with
# request/response traffic all session). Every ArduSub session — default
# GPS_TYPE, then GPS_TYPE=1 (AUTO), then GPS_TYPE=100 (SITL direct) — logs
# "GPS 1: probing for u-blox/SITL at 230400 baud" exactly once and then NEVER
# advances to "EKF3 IMUx is using GPS", no matter how long it runs. The
# equivalent DjiMatrice/Copter session, same --model JSON setup, logs the
# identical "probing for u-blox" line and completes in ~75s. So this is a
# real ArduSub-specific GPS backend-detection bug in this environment, not an
# EKF-tuning problem — none of EK3_SRC1_POSXY (already GPS, framework
# default, confirmed no ArduSub override exists), the EKF-health params
# above, nor GPS_TYPE 1 vs 100 change this outcome.
#
# Workaround, not a real fix: GPS_TYPE=14 (MAV) is the one GPS type whose
# backend-selection (AP_GPS.cpp's _detect_instance()) explicitly bypasses the
# stuck serial-probe path entirely — it just waits for MAVLink GPS_INPUT
# (#232) messages instead. t2_gps_input_bridge_bluerov2.py closes the loop:
# reads this same vehicle's own GLOBAL_POSITION_INT (already confirmed
# reliable) and relays it straight back as a synthetic GPS_INPUT. Must be
# run alongside the SITL/BiguaSim processes — see that script's docstring.
GPS1_TYPE 14
GPS_TYPE 14

# Fourth exception: position-hold overshoot found live once the BiguaSim
# buoyancy bug was fixed (see ~/biguasim's uuv.py -- peak buoyancy was
# ~3.1x the vehicle's own weight, an excess force far beyond its thrust
# authority, which caused a genuine surface-breach limit cycle no PID could
# hold against). With that fixed (near-neutral buoyancy, ~2N excess), the
# vehicle no longer porpoises out of the water, but still badly overshoots
# a commanded depth/position target -- confirmed live: a -3.0m depth
# target overshot to -7.55m before recovering, and a 5m-north/0m-east
# target overshot to ~9.3m north/-20.8m east and held there instead of
# correcting back. ArduSub's stock ArduSub-specific PSC gains (Parameters
# read directly from AC_PosControl.cpp: PSC_D_POS_P=3.0, PSC_D_VEL_P=8.0,
# PSC_NE_POS_P=1.0 defaults) are tuned for a real BlueROV2's real thrust/
# drag response -- BiguaSim's simulated dynamics apparently respond
# differently (faster/less damped), so the same gains produce a classic
# underdamped step response with integrator windup. Halved the vertical
# gains and mildly reduced the horizontal position gain as a first,
# conservative pass -- NOT yet confirmed live, needs a fresh test run.
PSC_D_POS_P 1.5
PSC_D_VEL_P 4.0
PSC_NE_POS_P 0.7

# Halving the P gains above barely reduced the overshoot (6.6m peak vs
# 7.55m before, same order of magnitude) -- confirmed live. Re-checked
# which integral term is actually live: PSC_D_VEL_I defaults to 0.0f
# (AC_PosControl.cpp:360, `_pid_vel_d_m(POSCONTROL_D_VEL_P, 0.0f, 0.0f,
# 0.0f, ...)`), not overridden by ArduPilot's own default_params/sub.parm
# either -- cutting it would be a no-op. The integral terms actually in
# effect come from that same sub.parm (loaded as the base layer before
# this file): PSC_D_ACC_I=0.4 (vertical acceleration loop) and
# PSC_NE_VEL_I=0.5 (horizontal velocity loop), both real, both plausible
# contributors to the windup-driven overshoot. Cutting both as the next
# test.
PSC_D_ACC_I 0.1
PSC_NE_VEL_I 0.15

# Confirmed live: cutting the integral terms above barely changed the
# overshoot either (~7.0m peak, same order of magnitude again) -- three
# separate PID-gain interventions in a row (P halved, I cut) all failed to
# meaningfully help, which is itself evidence the overshoot isn't really a
# feedback-gain problem. Checked the feedforward trajectory shaping
# instead: WP_SPD (`AC_WPNav.cpp`, `WP_SPD_DEFAULT = 10.0f`) is ArduSub's
# compiled default *horizontal* cruise speed between waypoints -- 10 m/s,
# not overridden anywhere (checked ArduPilot's own default_params/sub.parm
# too). Braking distance from 10 m/s at the default WP_ACC=2.5 m/s^2 is
# v^2/(2a) = 20 metres -- four times this test's entire 5m displacement.
# No tracking-PID gain can fix an overshoot whose root cause is the
# feedforward speed profile itself commanding a cruise speed the vehicle
# can't stop from in the available distance. (The vertical equivalent,
# WP_SPD_DN=1.5 m/s @ WP_ACC=2.5 m/s^2, only gives a ~0.45m braking
# distance -- doesn't by itself explain the ~7m depth overshoot, so the
# depth axis likely has a separate/additional cause not yet found.)
WP_SPD 1.0

ATC_RAT_RLL_P 0.1
ATC_RAT_RLL_I 0.05
ATC_RAT_RLL_D 0.001
ATC_ANG_RLL_P 3.0

# 2026-08-29: with the motor-mixing sign bugs actually fixed (uuv.py's
# ft_x/ft_y/ft_z, not motor_signs -- see biguasim_bridge/README.md), the
# depth overshoot is no longer confounded by wrong-direction thrust.
# Confirmed live: with correct signs, depth descended smoothly and
# correctly toward the -3.0m target (-1.89 -> -2.41m, clean, uncorrupted
# telemetry from before any collision) -- but still overshot past target
# and hit the world's riverbed (~-9.7m below spawn) before stopping. This
# is a real, single-direction (not oscillating) overshoot: the vehicle
# doesn't decelerate soon enough approaching the target. Cut the vertical
# position/velocity P gains further (already halved once, still not
# enough) and added a velocity D-term for damping (was 0 -- PSC_D_VEL_D
# defaults to 0.0f, AC_PosControl.cpp:360, no derivative damping on this
# loop at all before this). Also capped the max descent rate explicitly
# (WP_SPD_DN, compiled default 1.5 m/s, not previously overridden) so
# there's less momentum to arrest even if the gains still aren't perfect.
PSC_D_POS_P 0.6
PSC_D_VEL_P 1.5
PSC_D_VEL_D 0.3
WP_SPD_DN 0.5

# 2026-08-29: fixed a second, separate motor-mixing sign bug -- mt_z (yaw
# moment), not just ft_x/ft_y/ft_z (translation) above. Root-caused by
# tracing motor_mapping=[5,4,1,0,3,2] through ArduSub's own
# SUB_FRAME_VECTORED yaw_fac table (AP_Motors6DOF.cpp): the original
# mt_z = (M[2]-M[3]-M[4]+M[5])*sqrt came out with the OPPOSITE sign of
# ArduSub's own commanded yaw direction (confirmed both algebraically and
# with an isolated no-SITL numeric test) -- flipped in uuv.py the same
# decoupled way as ft_z (biguasim, not this repo).
#
# Confirmed live this changed the failure mode: before the fix, heading
# oscillated wildly both directions and never converged (inverted-sign
# feedback signature). After the fix, it instead spun up smoothly in ONE
# direction (angular_vel.z climbing 0->429 deg/s, no oscillation back) until
# SITL itself crashed -- the classic signature of a correctly-signed but
# completely undamped rate loop, not a sign error anymore. Checked:
# ATC_RAT_YAW_D=0.0 (AC_ATC_MULTI_RATE_YAW_D compiled default, confirmed via
# AC_AttitudeControl_Multi.h -- never overridden, unlike roll which already
# has ATC_RAT_RLL_D=0.001 above) -- zero derivative damping on yaw rate.
#
# Scaled yaw gains by the same ratios already validated live for roll
# (tuned/default): RAT_P 0.1/0.135=0.74, RAT_I 0.05/0.135=0.37,
# ANG_P 3.0/6.0=0.5 (ArduSub's Sub-specific AC_ATC_SUB_ANGLE_P=6.0 default,
# AC_AttitudeControl_Sub.cpp, not Multi's generic 4.5 -- confirmed both
# roll and yaw share this Sub-specific override). RAT_D has no default to
# scale from (compiled default is 0.0, unlike roll's 0.0036) -- borrowed
# roll's own tuned absolute value (0.001) as a first-pass damping term,
# since it's the only value already validated on this same vehicle/sim.
# NOT YET CONFIRMED LIVE.
ATC_RAT_YAW_P 0.13
ATC_RAT_YAW_I 0.007
ATC_RAT_YAW_D 0.001
ATC_ANG_YAW_P 3.0

# 2026-08-29: confirmed live the yaw-gain pass above genuinely slowed the
# spin-up (angular_vel.z reached only ~56 deg/s at the same point in the
# approach where it had already passed 100+ deg/s before), but the vehicle
# still overshot its depth target and hit the riverbed while still
# spinning, producing a violent multi-axis angular spike (14.97 -18.84
# 14.86 deg/s across x/y/z, not just yaw) that crashed SITL. Prioritizing
# the vertical overshoot first so there's no floor impact left to trigger
# that crash while the yaw damping is tuned further.
#
# Cut the position/velocity feedback gains again (same direction as the
# 2026-08-29 pass above, still not enough) and, new this pass: WP_ACC_Z --
# the vertical equivalent of WP_ACC, AC_WPNav.cpp's compiled default 1.0
# m/s² (confirmed via AC_WPNav.h's WP_ACC_Z_DEFAULT), never before
# overridden here. Together with the already-cut WP_SPD_DN, this shortens
# both the cruise speed and the braking runway needed to arrest it, mirror
# of the WP_SPD fix that worked for the horizontal axis. NOT YET CONFIRMED
# LIVE.
PSC_D_POS_P 0.3
PSC_D_VEL_P 0.8
PSC_D_VEL_D 0.5
WP_SPD_DN 0.3
WP_ACC_Z 0.3

# 2026-08-29: the first yaw-gain pass above (RAT_P/I/D, ANG_P) genuinely
# slowed the spiral (confirmed live) but didn't stop it -- angular_vel.z
# was still climbing (~56 deg/s and rising) when the vehicle hit the
# riverbed. That pass only reshaped the RATE-feedback response; it never
# capped how fast the controller is *allowed* to accelerate the commanded
# yaw rate in the first place. Found that cap: ATC_ACC_Y_MAX
# (AC_AttitudeControl.cpp's ACC_Y_MAX, backed by
# AC_ATC_SUB_ACCEL_Y_MAX_DEGSS=1100 deg/s^2 for Sub specifically --
# AC_AttitudeControl_Sub.cpp/.h) -- never overridden, and 1100 deg/s^2 is
# so loose it was never the binding constraint (the observed ~56 deg/s in
# under a second implies an effective accel far below that ceiling, so the
# runaway was coming from the feedback loop itself, not being reined in by
# any hard limit). Capping it well below what was actually observed forces
# the controller to ramp yaw rate up gradually no matter what the feedback
# loop is doing -- same "cap the commanded profile directly" strategy
# already validated for WP_SPD (horizontal) and WP_ACC_Z (vertical) above.
#
# Also strengthened the rate-loop pass itself: D raised from 0.001
# (borrowed from roll, evidently not enough) to 0.003 (close to Copter/Sub's
# own compiled roll/pitch default of 0.0036, AC_ATC_MULTI_RATE_RP_D --  a
# physically-plausible damping magnitude for this vehicle class rather than
# an arbitrary guess), and ANG_YAW_P cut further (3.0 -> 2.0) to slow the
# outer loop from demanding a fast rate change in the first place. NOT YET
# CONFIRMED LIVE.
ATC_RAT_YAW_D 0.003
ATC_ANG_YAW_P 2.0
ATC_ACC_Y_MAX 45.0

# 2026-08-29: root-caused the actual DRIVER of the sustained yaw spin, not
# just its symptom. It isn't the mixer anymore (mt_z's sign bug above is
# real and fixed) -- it's ArduSub's own default GUIDED auto-yaw behavior.
# Confirmed via source: WP_YAW_BEHAVIOR_DEFAULT = WP_YAW_BEHAVIOR_CORRECT_XTRACK
# (ArduSub/config.h), which ModeGuided::get_default_auto_yaw_mode()
# (mode_guided.cpp) turns into AUTO_YAW_CORRECT_XTRACK -- GUIDED continuously
# re-points the vehicle to correct cross-track error toward the target.
# ardupilot_interface.cpp's GlobalPositionTarget always sets
# IGNORE_YAW|IGNORE_YAW_RATE (never commands yaw explicitly), so this
# default behavior is what's actually driving yaw here. The horizontal
# position/velocity gains (PSC_NE_POS_P/PSC_NE_VEL_I above) are still much
# closer to their defaults than the vertical ones (which got several
# aggressive cuts this session) -- if horizontal position is
# overshooting/noisy, the cross-track error vector swings, and
# CORRECT_XTRACK chases it, producing the observed sustained one-direction
# spin. The RAT_YAW_D/ANG_YAW_P/ACC_Y_MAX changes above only damp the
# symptom; this cuts it at the source for this hold-test (heading doesn't
# matter here). NOT YET CONFIRMED LIVE.
WP_YAW_BEHAVIOR 0

# 2026-08-30: re-enabling FS_EKF_ACTION (was 0/disabled, see the original
# reasoning near the top of this file -- kept for history, not wrong, just
# superseded). It was disabled because BiguaSim's synthetic GPS produced a
# transient jump/glitch right around AHRS init that tripped the EKF check
# and silently blocked every GUIDED mode-set request. That specific glitch
# is now covered by EK3_CHECK_SCALE/EK3_GLITCH_RAD above (glitch-RADIUS
# tolerance, a different EKF metric from what FS_EKF_ACTION reacts to --
# ahrs.get_variances()'s compass/velocity variance, ArduSub/failsafe.cpp's
# failsafe_ekf_check()), so re-enabling shouldn't reintroduce the original
# arming problem.
#
# Motivation: confirmed live this session that the vehicle can rise
# uncommanded (all the way to the surface) during a suspected Holodeck
# physics stall -- see runner.py's new env.step() watchdog log for direct
# evidence next time this happens. Sub's EKF failsafe only has two real
# options (ArduSub/defines.h): FS_EKF_ACTION_DISABLED=0 or
# FS_EKF_ACTION_DISARM=2 -- no passive "hold position" action exists for
# Sub (unlike Copter's LAND/ALTHOLD options), so DISARM is the only
# available protection. A prolonged loss of fresh EKF corrections should
# show up as growing compass/velocity variance (EKF covariance grows during
# prediction-only steps with no measurement updates) and cross
# FS_EKF_THRESH (left at its compiled default -- not yet tuned) after 2
# continuous seconds of bad variance -- catching a real stall regardless of
# its root cause, not just the one bridge-freeze mechanism investigated
# this session. Disarming cuts thrust rather than continuing to apply a
# stale, possibly-wrong command -- with buoyancy now much closer to neutral
# (uuv.py's 1.002 multiplier), a disarmed drift should be far gentler than
# letting a stale non-corrective command keep running indefinitely.
# NOT YET CONFIRMED LIVE -- watch for both false positives (disarming
# during a normal transient) and whether FS_EKF_THRESH's default is even
# the right sensitivity for this simulated GPS/EKF setup.
#
# UPDATE: FS_EKF_ACTION_DISARM=2 reproduced the same SITL boot crash
# ("Barometer 2 calib[535/740]" then "Connection reset by peer") 3/3 times
# -- confirmed real, not environmental flakiness. Re-read AP_Arming::disarm()
# (AP_Arming.cpp) -- it early-returns safely if not armed
# (`if (!armed) return false;`), which is exactly the state during boot/baro
# calibration, so the crash isn't the obvious top-level guard; something
# deeper in that call path (or a race on the armed flag itself this early
# in boot) is still unexplained. Not worth chasing further right now --
# ArduSub/defines.h has a third, actually-passive option that sidesteps the
# whole disarm() call: FS_EKF_ACTION_WARN_ONLY=1 ("Send warning to gcs").
# failsafe_ekf_check() (failsafe.cpp) runs the identical variance-check/
# logging/GCS-warning path for both WARN_ONLY and DISARM -- the disarm()
# call is the ONLY thing DISARM adds, so WARN_ONLY should get the same
# detection (dataflash ERR entry + GCS "EKF bad" text) without the crash
# risk. Doesn't cut thrust during a real stall (no passive "hold" action
# exists for Sub), but gives clean, unambiguous confirmation next time a
# stall-like EKF-variance event happens. NOT YET CONFIRMED LIVE.
# UPDATE 2: FS_EKF_ACTION_WARN_ONLY=1 reproduced the identical boot crash
# too -- same symptom (no new dataflash log, MAVROS "channel closed", ardusub
# process alive but never stabilizes a connection). Since WARN_ONLY never
# calls arming.disarm() at all, this rules out the disarm() call as the
# cause -- it's something in the CODE PATH SHARED by both nonzero actions
# (ahrs.get_variances(), LOGGER_WRITE_ERROR, or gcs().send_text() inside
# failsafe_ekf_check()) that doesn't tolerate being exercised this early in
# boot (before AHRS/logging is fully up) in this ArduSub V4.8.0-dev build.
# 3/3 reproductions across both nonzero values, 0 reliable otherwise -- this
# is a real upstream ArduSub bug, not something fixable from this repo's
# side (would need patching ArduPilot's C++ failsafe_ekf_check() itself).
# Reverted back to disabled; abandoning FS_EKF_ACTION as the mitigation for
# the slow-drift-during-a-stall problem -- the runner.py env.step() watchdog
# (BiguaSim side) remains the only instrumentation for that.
FS_EKF_ACTION 0

# 2026-08-30: root-caused both the yaw divergence and the slow rise as two
# SEPARATE mechanisms, from a raw motor/quaternion log of a run that ran
# long enough (66s) to actually diverge (earlier ~20-30s tests just cut off
# before it got bad -- the yaw was never actually stable, just slow).
#
# YAW: confirmed with real logged motor values (t=19.02s: m2=-71.8, m3=-64.8,
# m4=68.3, m5=66.9 -- NOT symmetric for a nominally pure-forward command) that
# mt_z comes out nonzero even with no explicit yaw commanded. Root cause: TT
# (and M) scale with rotor_speed^2 (uuv.py), but ArduSub's mixer sums
# corrections LINEARLY assuming thrust is proportional to command. Riding on
# top of a large forward-thrust common-mode bias, any ordinary small
# rate-controller correction becomes an asymmetric perturbation that the
# squaring amplifies disproportionately into real yaw torque -- which
# demands more correction, which gets amplified more -- a genuine positive-
# feedback loop, not a simple constant-bias problem (matches the observed
# shape: slow buildup over ~30s, then a sudden runaway to a full tumble by
# t=50-56s, all while the watchdog logged zero stalls -- confirmed real
# physics, not a Holodeck freeze). Attacking the amplitude available to that
# loop rather than chasing gains further: less forward bias to begin with,
# and a smoother ramp so corrections stay small.
WP_SPD 0.5
WP_ACC 1.0
ATC_ACC_Y_MAX 15.0

# 2026-08-30 update: confirmed live the cuts above only DELAYED the yaw
# divergence (~50s -> ~55s), didn't eliminate it -- reverse-solved the real
# per-motor forward/lateral/yaw decomposition from a logged sample
# (t=19.02s: m2=-71.8,m3=-64.8,m4=68.3,m5=66.9 -> Forward~68, Lateral~1.4,
# YAW~2.1) and confirmed a genuine, nonzero yaw command is already present
# minutes before any visible divergence -- not sensor noise, a real
# correction that the v^2 coupling (see above) amplifies over time. Cutting
# much harder this pass instead of incrementally: less cruise speed left to
# feed the loop, a much tighter yaw-accel ceiling, and more rate damping
# (ATC_RAT_YAW_D pushed past roll/pitch's own compiled default now that
# live evidence shows this axis is measurably more sensitive to the
# coupling than roll turned out to be).
WP_SPD 0.2
ATC_ACC_Y_MAX 5.0
ATC_RAT_YAW_D 0.01

# RISE: PSC_D_VEL_I (vertical velocity loop integral gain) has been 0.0
# (compiled default, AC_PosControl.cpp:360) this entire investigation --
# only P and D were ever tuned. A pure P(D) controller mathematically CANNOT
# fully null a constant force disturbance (whatever residual buoyancy excess
# remains, even the small ~0.2N left after uuv.py's 1.002 multiplier) --
# it always settles at a nonzero steady-state position error proportional to
# the disturbance. That's the actual, general-purpose fix for "still rises,
# just slower/further out now matter how small the excess is" -- an
# integral term is the standard control-theory answer for exactly this,
# rather than continuing to chase smaller and smaller buoyancy margins.
PSC_D_VEL_I 0.15

# 2026-08-30: same fix pattern as PSC_D_VEL_I above, applied to the last
# residual -- yaw is now down to a slow, roughly-constant ~0.04 deg/s creep
# (confirmed live: 358.7->358.0 over 17s, no acceleration, not the old
# positive-feedback runaway -- that mechanism is gone, this is just a small
# steady disturbance getting through). ATC_RAT_YAW_I was set to 0.007 early
# in this session's yaw work, before the root cause was understood -- never
# revisited since. A small, steady residual is exactly what a rate-loop
# integral term is for; bumping it meaningfully (not another marginal
# nudge) to actually null it out over time instead of just damping/capping
# around it. NOT YET CONFIRMED LIVE.
ATC_RAT_YAW_I 0.04

# 2026-08-30: yaw itself held rock-steady this pass (358-360 deg for 20+
# real-time seconds, confirmed live) -- the ATC_RAT_YAW_I bump worked. But
# the same run still tumbled, just PITCH led it this time instead of yaw
# (Pitch: 0.15 -> -0.25 -> 0.68 -> 1.33 -> -9.62 -> -18.05 deg,
# Roll dragged along right behind it -- confirmed live via dataflash ATT).
# Checked: ATC_RAT_PIT_P/D are still completely untouched (0.135/0.0036,
# Multi's raw compiled default) -- the same starting state roll was in
# before its own (successful, if more modest) tuning pass early this
# session. Applying the same recipe that worked best overall (yaw's more
# thorough pass, not roll's lighter one) to pitch now: cut P, add real D
# damping (was 0 in practice -- 0.0036 is negligible), cut the outer
# angle-P so it doesn't demand fast rate changes in the first place.
# NOT YET CONFIRMED LIVE.
ATC_RAT_PIT_P 0.1
ATC_RAT_PIT_D 0.01
ATC_ANG_PIT_P 3.0

# 2026-08-30 (run 2 of the 3-clean-runs bar): the pitch gain pass above
# reduced severity/frequency (one clean 180s run) but did not eliminate the
# instability -- confirmed live in a second run: Pitch grew 4.50 -> 8.00 ->
# 11.15 -> 12.26 -> -36.15 -> -55.48 deg while STILL in GUIDED (MODE log:
# POSHOLD only engaged afterward, at the tail end of an already-severe
# tumble -- POSHOLD inherited it, didn't cause it, same as every other
# occurrence). Found the roll/pitch equivalent of ATC_ACC_Y_MAX (which
# helped yaw): ATC_ACC_R_MAX/ATC_ACC_P_MAX, both defaulting to 1100 deg/s^2
# (AC_ATTITUDE_CONTROL_ACCEL_RP_MAX_DEFAULT_DEGSS, AC_AttitudeControl.h) --
# never touched, same loose-non-binding-ceiling situation yaw's own default
# was in before capping it helped. Applying the same hard ceiling to both
# axes now instead of pushing D/P further -- this run's escalation
# (4->8->11->12 deg over ~4s, then a much faster final blowup) looks exactly
# like the shape a runaway takes before a rate cap intervenes, not
# something P/D alone reliably out-damps once triggered.
ATC_ACC_R_MAX 20.0
ATC_ACC_P_MAX 20.0
